1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,542
Welcome to Algebra Two. In Unit One, we'll
review functions and learn to transform

2
00:00:05,542 --> 00:00:08,043
them. We start with parent functions.

3
00:00:09,167 --> 00:00:12,601
A parent function is the simplest function
of a family.

4
00:00:13,051 --> 00:00:18,035
Every other function in the family is the
parent shifted, stretched or flipped.

5
00:00:18,035 --> 00:00:21,819
Knowing the parents' shapes lets you
sketch almost anything.

6
00:00:22,967 --> 00:00:28,005
The linear parent is y equals x: a
straight line through the origin with

7
00:00:28,005 --> 00:00:28,705
slope one.

8
00:00:29,155 --> 00:00:34,846
The quadratic parent is y equals x
squared: a parabola with its vertex at the

9
00:00:34,846 --> 00:00:35,363
origin.

10
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:42,205
The cubic parent, y equals x cubed, rises
from the bottom left to the top right,

11
00:00:42,205 --> 00:00:43,988
flattening at the origin.

12
00:00:44,438 --> 00:00:50,154
The absolute value parent, y equals the
absolute value of x, is a V with its

13
00:00:50,154 --> 00:00:51,734
corner at the origin.

14
00:00:52,867 --> 00:00:58,952
The square root parent, y equals root x,
starts at the origin and rises slowly to

15
00:00:58,952 --> 00:01:03,235
the right. Its domain is x greater than or
equal to zero.

16
00:01:03,685 --> 00:01:09,204
The reciprocal parent, y equals one over
x, has two branches, and never touches

17
00:01:09,204 --> 00:01:10,042
either axis.

18
00:01:11,167 --> 00:01:17,025
The exponential parent, y equals two to
the x, passes through zero comma one, and

19
00:01:17,025 --> 00:01:18,761
grows faster and faster.

20
00:01:19,211 --> 00:01:22,945
On the left, it approaches the x axis but
never reaches it.

21
00:01:24,067 --> 00:01:29,255
Your turn. Which parent function has
domain x greater than or equal to zero,

22
00:01:29,255 --> 00:01:31,576
and passes through four comma two?

23
00:01:32,026 --> 00:01:33,562
Pause the video and try it.

24
00:01:34,012 --> 00:01:37,127
The square root function: the square root
of four is two.

25
00:01:38,266 --> 00:01:42,809
In this lesson, we'll transform parent
functions with shifts, stretches and

26
00:01:42,809 --> 00:01:43,535
reflections.

27
00:01:44,666 --> 00:01:50,442
Starting from f of x, the transformed
function is a times f of the quantity x

28
00:01:50,442 --> 00:01:51,642
minus h, plus k.

29
00:01:52,092 --> 00:01:57,356
h moves the graph left or right, k moves
it up or down, and a stretches or flips

30
00:01:57,356 --> 00:01:57,553
it.

31
00:01:58,699 --> 00:02:02,134
f of x minus h shifts the graph h units
right.

32
00:02:02,584 --> 00:02:08,871
The square root of x minus three starts at
three comma zero instead of the origin. A

33
00:02:08,871 --> 00:02:11,565
plus sign inside means a shift left.

34
00:02:12,699 --> 00:02:15,835
f of x plus k shifts the graph k units up.

35
00:02:16,285 --> 00:02:20,659
The absolute value of x, minus two, is the
V moved down two.

36
00:02:21,799 --> 00:02:27,522
a times f of x stretches vertically when a
is greater than one, and compresses when a

37
00:02:27,522 --> 00:02:29,138
is between zero and one.

38
00:02:29,588 --> 00:02:34,516
Three x squared is narrower than x
squared. One half x squared is wider.

39
00:02:35,666 --> 00:02:42,296
A negative a reflects the graph across the
x axis. f of negative x reflects it across

40
00:02:42,296 --> 00:02:43,154
the y axis.

41
00:02:43,604 --> 00:02:48,873
Negative root x opens downward. Root of
negative x opens to the left.

42
00:02:49,999 --> 00:02:56,067
Your turn. Describe the transformations in
g of x equals negative two times the

43
00:02:56,067 --> 00:02:59,215
absolute value of x plus one, plus three.

44
00:02:59,665 --> 00:03:01,201
Pause the video and try it.

45
00:03:01,651 --> 00:03:07,793
Shift left one, stretch by two, reflect
across the x axis, and shift up three. The

46
00:03:07,793 --> 00:03:12,062
vertex is negative one comma three, and
the V opens down.

47
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:18,327
Some functions follow different rules on
different parts of their domain. In this

48
00:03:18,327 --> 00:03:20,859
lesson, we'll study piecewise functions.

49
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,652
A piecewise function lists several
formulas, each with the interval where it

50
00:03:26,652 --> 00:03:27,141
applies.

51
00:03:27,591 --> 00:03:34,334
For example, f of x equals x plus two when
x is less than one, and three minus x when

52
00:03:34,334 --> 00:03:35,762
x is at least one.

53
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:40,463
To evaluate, first find which piece the
input belongs to.

54
00:03:40,913 --> 00:03:47,332
f of negative two uses the first piece:
negative two plus two is zero. f of four

55
00:03:47,332 --> 00:03:51,345
uses the second: three minus four is
negative one.

56
00:03:52,467 --> 00:03:55,432
To graph, draw each piece only on its own
interval.

57
00:03:55,882 --> 00:04:02,029
The line x plus two runs up to x equals
one. The line three minus x starts at x

58
00:04:02,029 --> 00:04:04,053
equals one and goes right.

59
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:10,766
At a boundary, use an open circle if the
point isn't included, and a closed dot if

60
00:04:10,766 --> 00:04:11,173
it is.

61
00:04:11,623 --> 00:04:17,689
At x equals one, the first piece has an
open circle at one comma three. The second

62
00:04:17,689 --> 00:04:20,647
piece has a closed dot at one comma two.

63
00:04:21,767 --> 00:04:26,268
Piecewise functions model things like
shipping costs or tax brackets.

64
00:04:26,718 --> 00:04:32,021
For example, shipping costs five dollars
for up to two pounds, and five dollars

65
00:04:32,021 --> 00:04:34,974
plus two dollars per extra pound after
that.

66
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:42,814
Your turn. For f of x equals x squared if
x is less than zero, and two x plus one if

67
00:04:42,814 --> 00:04:47,449
x is at least zero, find f of negative
three and f of two.

68
00:04:47,899 --> 00:04:49,435
Pause the video and try it.

69
00:04:49,885 --> 00:04:53,192
f of negative three is nine. f of two is
five.

70
00:04:54,333 --> 00:04:59,655
Absolute value functions make V shapes. In
this lesson, we'll graph them and find

71
00:04:59,655 --> 00:05:00,904
their key features.

72
00:05:02,033 --> 00:05:07,926
The parent y equals the absolute value of
x has its vertex at the origin, with

73
00:05:07,926 --> 00:05:10,268
slopes of one and negative one.

74
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:17,458
The vertex form is y equals a times the
absolute value of x minus h, plus k.

75
00:05:17,908 --> 00:05:23,754
The vertex is h comma k, and a sets the
slopes of the two sides: a and negative a.

76
00:05:24,900 --> 00:05:30,574
Graph y equals two times the absolute
value of x minus one, minus four.

77
00:05:31,024 --> 00:05:36,544
The vertex is one comma negative four.
From there, go up two for every one step,

78
00:05:36,544 --> 00:05:37,510
left or right.

79
00:05:38,633 --> 00:05:45,409
To find the x intercepts, set y equal to
zero. Two times the absolute value of x

80
00:05:45,409 --> 00:05:47,273
minus one equals four.

81
00:05:47,723 --> 00:05:54,255
The absolute value of x minus one is two,
so x equals three or negative one. The y

82
00:05:54,255 --> 00:05:59,115
intercept is two times one minus four,
which is negative two.

83
00:06:00,233 --> 00:06:03,497
Every absolute value function is really
piecewise.

84
00:06:03,947 --> 00:06:10,630
The absolute value of x is x when x is at
least zero, and negative x when x is less

85
00:06:10,630 --> 00:06:11,435
than zero.

86
00:06:12,566 --> 00:06:18,350
Your turn. Find the vertex and x
intercepts of y equals the absolute value

87
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:20,460
of x plus two, minus three.

88
00:06:20,910 --> 00:06:22,446
Pause the video and try it.

89
00:06:22,896 --> 00:06:29,160
The vertex is negative two comma negative
three. The absolute value of x plus two

90
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:32,794
equals three, so x equals one or negative
five.

91
00:06:33,933 --> 00:06:38,882
In this lesson, we'll feed one function
into another. That's called composition.

92
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,800
f of g of x means: first apply g, then
apply f to the result.

93
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:50,249
It's written f circle g. Think of a
machine whose output goes straight into a

94
00:06:50,249 --> 00:06:51,223
second machine.

95
00:06:52,366 --> 00:06:59,300
Let f of x equal x squared, and g of x
equal x plus three. Find f of g of two.

96
00:06:59,750 --> 00:07:03,270
g of two is five. Then f of five is
twenty-five.

97
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:07,109
Now find the formula for f of g of x.

98
00:07:07,559 --> 00:07:13,566
Replace the x in f with g of x: the
quantity x plus three, squared. That's x

99
00:07:13,566 --> 00:07:15,858
squared plus six x plus nine.

100
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,821
Order matters. g of f of x is x squared
plus three, which is different.

101
00:07:22,271 --> 00:07:27,604
Check at two: g of f of two is four plus
three, which is seven, not twenty-five.

102
00:07:28,733 --> 00:07:34,645
You can also break a function apart. h of
x equals the square root of two x plus

103
00:07:34,645 --> 00:07:34,941
one.

104
00:07:35,391 --> 00:07:42,692
That's f of g of x, with g of x equals two
x plus one inside, and f of x equals root

105
00:07:42,692 --> 00:07:43,562
x outside.

106
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:51,512
Your turn. With f of x equals two x minus
one and g of x equals x squared, find f of

107
00:07:51,512 --> 00:07:54,108
g of three, and g of f of three.

108
00:07:54,558 --> 00:07:56,094
Pause the video and try it.

109
00:07:56,544 --> 00:08:03,049
f of g of three is f of nine, which is
seventeen. g of f of three is g of five,

110
00:08:03,049 --> 00:08:04,778
which is twenty-five.

111
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:11,084
In this last lesson of Unit One, we'll
learn inverse functions, which undo what a

112
00:08:11,084 --> 00:08:11,980
function does.

113
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:17,217
If f takes three to seven, its inverse
takes seven back to three.

114
00:08:17,667 --> 00:08:21,870
The inverse is written f inverse. Inputs
and outputs swap.

115
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:29,059
To find an inverse, write y equals f of x,
swap x and y, and solve for y.

116
00:08:29,509 --> 00:08:36,143
For f of x equals two x plus three: x
equals two y plus three, so y equals x

117
00:08:36,143 --> 00:08:38,063
minus three, over two.

118
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:43,253
Check by composing. f of f inverse of x
should equal x.

119
00:08:43,703 --> 00:08:49,677
Two times the quantity x minus three over
two, plus three, is x minus three plus

120
00:08:49,677 --> 00:08:51,767
three, which is x. It works.

121
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:58,669
The graph of an inverse is the reflection
of the original across the line y equals

122
00:08:58,669 --> 00:08:58,809
x.

123
00:08:59,259 --> 00:09:03,590
Every point a comma b on f becomes b comma
a on f inverse.

124
00:09:04,733 --> 00:09:08,467
Only one-to-one functions have inverses
that are functions.

125
00:09:08,917 --> 00:09:14,102
Use the horizontal line test: if a
horizontal line hits the graph more than

126
00:09:14,102 --> 00:09:18,389
once, like with x squared, you must
restrict the domain first.

127
00:09:19,533 --> 00:09:24,248
Your turn. Find the inverse of f of x
equals x cubed minus one.

128
00:09:24,698 --> 00:09:26,234
Pause the video and try it.

129
00:09:26,684 --> 00:09:32,849
Swap: x equals y cubed minus one. So y
equals the cube root of x plus one.

130
00:09:33,967 --> 00:09:39,653
That completes Unit One. Next, we'll start
Unit Two: equations and systems. If this

131
00:09:39,653 --> 00:09:44,655
helped, subscribe to School Math,
Explained, and turn on notifications so

132
00:09:44,655 --> 00:09:46,916
you don't miss it. See you there.

